      "writtenOn": "2024-05-14T02:30:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s right. If you look at the restorecon implementation, if a path matches, then the directory itself gets it security context updated but whatever is below is then skipped. The main difference between `/data/media` and `/data/system_ce` for instance, is that `/data/media` has the `obb` subdirectory which is not encrypted (FWIU). That\u0027s why we use `fnmatch` and not just `strncmp`.\n\nIf I unroll the restorecon implementation:\n/data/system_ce -\u003e is considered CE, its security context is restored but anything below is skipped.\n/data/system_ce/0 -\u003e while this is the encrypted directory, it has been skipped.\n/data/media -\u003e not considered CE, relabelled using standard rules.\n/data/media/0 -\u003e is considered CE, relabelled but skip any subdirectory.\n/data/media/obb -\u003e not considered CE, relabelled using standard rules.\n\nIf we wanted to be consistent, the function should probably return true only for `/data/system_ce/0` but I don\u0027t believe there is any subdirectory other than the user directories in `/data/system_ce` so that\u0027s why we have that shortcut.",
